  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1240 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nOverall, a fairly low impact weather regime will persist through \nlate week. Seasonably strong northwest flow aloft will continue \nthrough Thursday although the stronger instability and greater \nmoisture will remain south of the local area. Some small (20-40%) \nchances for showers/storms continue through Thursday, but synoptic \nforcing and low-level convergence are weak. The stronger flow aloft \nwill result in elongated hodographs, so an isolated strong storm\ncannot be ruled out through Wednesday, particularly south of \nhighway 29, where instability is generally higher. \n\nRain chances decrease further towards the end of the work week as \nmid-level heights build across the Upper Midwest. However, showers \nand storm chances (50-70%) will return to start the weekend as \na progressive shortwave trough moves across the region. \nPrecipitable water values increase above the 95th percentile \nahead of this system, but seasonably cool temperatures and \nrelatively weak instability look to limit the severe weather \nrisk, with little signal currently indicated in the machine \nlearning guidance. Generally drier and seasonable weather is \nforecast behind the weekend system. \n\nOtherwise, some fog development may occur tonight with light flow \nand a moist boundary layer. At least locally dense fog may occur, \nespecially given the favorable time of year with nights becoming \nlonger, but will have to watch cloud trends overnight.",
